  "summary": "Regional variant of Mannerist architecture in Tuscany characterized by exaggerated proportions, spatial tension, and deliberate distortion of classical forms.",
  "description": "Tuscan Mannerism emerged as a sophisticated reaction against High Renaissance harmony, introducing deliberate distortions, elongated proportions, and complex spatial arrangements that created visual tension and psychological impact. It reflected the intellectual climate of Florence during the late Renaissance and the influence of the Medici family's patronage.",
  "culturalContext": "Tuscan Mannerism — the anti-classical riposte of Michelangelo's followers — stretched Renaissance rules into virtuoso discomfort: Ammannati's courtyard at Palazzo Pitti, Buontalenti's grottoes and Boboli garden facades, Vasari's Uffizi corridor, rustic-stone rustication gone wild, splayed keystones, dropping swag pediments and broken cornices. Giorgio Vasari theorized it as grazia and difficultia; the style's engineered weirdness became the ground-plan of Baroque drama while remaining recognizably Florentine in its stone craft.",
